<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · War and march · 1956 · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p><h1 class="sg-title">Wallah Zaman, Ya Silahi</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="arz">والله زمان يا سلاحي</bdi> / <bdi lang="arz-Latn">Wallāh Zamān, Yā Silāḥī</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An Egyptian song sung by Umm Kulthum during the Suez war of 1956, which became the national anthem of Egypt and of other Arab states.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Salah Jahin</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Kamal Al Taweel</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Egypt and the United Arab Republic; also Iraq and Libya</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Egyptian Arabic</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Egypt</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1956; national anthem from 1960</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem · War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>In copyright (words by Salah Jahin, music by Kamal Al Taweel, 1956)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallah-zaman-ya-silahi">Wallah Zaman, Ya Silahi</a>&quot; was first a nationalist song, written by Salah Jahin, set by Kamal Al Taweel and sung by Umm Kulthum during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-crisis">Suez Crisis</a> of 1956, known in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the Arab world as the Tripartite Aggression, when Egypt was invaded by the United Kingdom, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Israel. Its words evoke national resistance, and during the war Egyptian radio played it often, at times every ten minutes.</p><p>Its popularity made it the national anthem of the United Arab Republic, the union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, in 1960, two years after the union was founded. It replaced the anthems adopted after the Egyptian revolution of 1952 and the end of the monarchy: the former royal anthem &quot;Salam Afandina&quot;, composed by Giuseppe Pugioli, and &quot;Nashid al-Huriyya&quot;, composed and sung by Mohammed Abdel Wahab, as well as Syria's former anthem. The union broke up in 1961, but Egypt kept the name United Arab Republic until 1971 and the anthem until 1979; the Arabic article ties its official use to the years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser">Gamal Abdel Nasser</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> used it as its national anthem from 1963 to 1981, without words from 1965, and the Arabic article adds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> used it from 1969 to 1977. The words are usually sung in Egyptian Arabic rather than Modern Standard Arabic.</p><p>In 1979, during the peace negotiations with Israel, President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a> replaced it with the less militant &quot;Bilady, Bilady, Bilady&quot;, which remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s national anthem.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A patriotic war song of national resistance, written for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> under invasion; martial and stirring.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords" data-say>The words of this song are still in copyright, so they are not printed here. &#8220;What it says&#8221; above gives their theme in our own words, and the performance below lets you hear them.</p></section>
</div>
